      "text": "To solve the problem, we start by simplifying the expression \\(\\sqrt[4]{2^7 \\cdot 3^3}\\).\n\nFirst, we express the radicand in terms of powers of 2 and 3:\n\\[\n2^7 \\cdot 3^3 = (2^4 \\cdot 2^3) \\cdot (3^2 \\cdot 3) = 2^4 \\cdot 2^3 \\cdot 3^2 \\cdot 3 = 2^4 \\cdot 3^2 \\cdot 2^3 \\cdot 3 = 2^7 \\cdot 3^3\n\\]\n\nNow, we take the fourth root of the expression:\n\\[\n\\sqrt[4]{2^7 \\cdot 3^3} = \\sqrt[4]{2^7 \\cdot 3^3} = \\sqrt[4]{2^7} \\cdot \\sqrt[4]{3^3}\n\\]\n\nNext, we simplify \\(\\sqrt[4]{2^7}\\):\n\\[\n\\sqrt[4]{2^7} = 2^{7/4} = 2^{1 + 3/4} = 2 \\cdot 2^{3/4} = 2 \\sqrt[4]{8}\n\\]\n\nNow, we simplify \\(\\sqrt[4]{3^3}\\):\n\\[\n\\sqrt[4]{3^3} = 3^{3/4}\n\\]\n\nCombining these results, we get:\n\\[\n\\sqrt[4]{2^7 \\cdot 3^3} = 2 \\sqrt[4]{8} \\cdot 3^{3/4}\n\\]\n\nWe need to express \\(8\\) as a perfect fourth power:\n\\[\n8 = 2^3 = (2^4)^{3/4} = 2\n\\]\n\nThus, \\(\\sqrt[4]{8} = \\sqrt[4]{2^3}\\). However, we can rewrite \\(8\\) as \\(2^3\\) and recognize that:\n\\[\n\\sqrt[4]{2^3} = 2^{3/4}\n\\]\n\nSo, we have:\n\\[\n2 \\sqrt[4]{8} \\cdot 3^{3/4} = 2 \\cdot 2^{3/4} \\cdot 3^{3/4} = 2 \\cdot (2 \\cdot 3)^{3/4} = 2 \\cdot 6^{3/4}\n\\]\n\nWe can express \\(6\\) as \\(2 \\cdot 3\\), but it's simpler to keep it as \\(2 \\cdot 3^{3/4}\\):\n\\[\n2 \\cdot 3^{3/4}\n\\]\n\nThus, the expression \\(\\sqrt[4]{2^7 \\cdot 3^3}\\) simplifies to:\n\\[\n2 \\cdot 3^{3/4}\n\\]\n\nThis is in the form \\(a \\sqrt[4]{b}\\), where \\(a = 6\\) and \\(b = 8\\). Therefore, \\(a + b = 6 + 8 = 14\\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{14}\n\\]",
