      "text": "To simplify \\(\\sqrt[4]{2^7 \\cdot 3^3}\\), we start by expressing the radicand in a form that allows us to separate the perfect fourth powers from the terms that are not.\n\nGiven:\n\\[\n\\sqrt[4]{2^7 \\cdot 3^3}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we recognize that we can separate the terms inside the fourth root into part that is a perfect fourth power and another part that is not:\n\\[\n\\sqrt[4]{2^7 \\cdot 3^3} = \\sqrt[4]{2^4 \\cdot 2^3 \\cdot 3^3}\n\\]\n\nWe know that \\(2^4\\) is a perfect fourth power. Therefore, we can simplify this part:\n\\[\n\\sqrt[4]{2^4 \\cdot 2^3 \\cdot 3^3} = \\sqrt[4]{2^4} \\cdot \\sqrt[4]{2^3} \\cdot \\sqrt[4]{3^3}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\sqrt[4]{2^4} = 2\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\sqrt[4]{2^4 \\cdot 2^3 \\cdot 3^3} = 2 \\cdot \\sqrt[4]{2^3 \\cdot 3^3}\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to simplify \\(\\sqrt[4]{2^3 \\cdot 3^3}\\). We recognize that \\(\\sqrt[4]{2^3} = 2^{3/4}\\) and \\(\\sqrt[4]{3^3} = 3^{3/4}\\). Therefore:\n\\[\n2 \\cdot \\sqrt[4]{2^3 \\cdot 3^3} = 2 \\cdot 2^{3/4} \\cdot 3^{3/4}\n\\]\n\nWe can combine the exponents using the property of exponents \\(a^m \\cdot a^n = a^{m+n}\\):\n\\[\n2 \\cdot 2^{3/4} \\cdot 3^{3/4} = 2^{1 + 3/4} \\cdot 3^{3/4} = 2^{7/4} \\cdot 3^{3/4}\n\\]\n\nThus, the simplified form of \\(\\sqrt[4]{2^7 \\cdot 3^3}\\) is \\(2^{7/4} \\cdot 3^{3/4}\\), which can be written as:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot \\sqrt[4]{2^3 \\cdot 3^3}} = \\boxed{6 \\cdot \\sqrt[4]{2^3 \\cdot 3^3}}\n\\]\n\nIn this form, \\(a = 6\\) and \\(b = 2^3 \\cdot 3^3 = 8 \\cdot 27 = 216\\), so \\(a + b = 6 + 216 = 222\\).\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{222}\\).",
